      "snippet": {
        "text": "Research has examined the potential of ChatGPT in mathematics education, highlighting its high accuracy in solving mathematical problems, especially those of lower difficulty levels (Dao & Le, 2023;Li et al., 2023). It can assist in teaching mathematical concepts, provide personalized learning experiences, and support self-regulated learning (Gouia-Zarrad & Gunn, 2024;Wardat et al., 2023). For instance, Li et al. (2023) found that ChatGPT has high accuracy in answering middle school math questions and could potentially enhance students' self-regulation in flipped math classrooms. Dao and Le (2023) reported that ChatGPT succeeds in providing responses to secondary mathematics topics such as exponential and logarithmic functions. While ChatGPT can be a valuable tool for fact-checking and knowledge retrieval, its overall mathematical performance is below graduate-level (Frieder et al., 2023). Furthermore, Urhan et al. (2024) found that ChatGPT did not consistently provide reliable reasoning to support students in learning calculus concepts. It also struggled with mathematical word problems, particularly when instructed not to show its solving process (Shakarian et al., 2023). Despite these challenges, studies suggest that ChatGPT could lead to a positive shift in student attitudes, classroom engagement, and improved learning outcomes in mathematics (Gouia-Zarrad & Gunn, 2024;Serhan & Welcome, 2024). For example, students reported increased interest and excitement when using ChatGPT for learning numerical methods and coding (Gouia-Zarrad & Gunn, 2024). \n\nCurrent studies in mathematics education have primarily focused on ChatGPT's abilities to solve mathematical problems and its capabilities to help students perform mathematical activities, such as constructing proofs. Some studies have explored students' perspectives on using ChatGPT to learn mathematics. For example, Serhan and Welcome (2024) examined student perceptions of using ChatGPT in learning 