      "snippet": {
        "text": "The study found that ChatGPT improved students' perceptions of mathematics, enhancing engagement and motivation, and led to notable advancements in academic achievements. Feedback indicated it fostered a more engaging, tailored learning environment with collaborative problem solving and real-time feedback, aiding understanding and retention. Moreover, ChatGPT allowed teachers to focus on higher-order discussions and innovative approaches, nurturing students to be more flexible, skilled, and confident, better preparing them for future challenges. It created a dynamic educational environment that improved mathematical proficiency and cultivated critical thinking and problem-solving abilities. \n\nChatGPT has also been used to generate tips in intelligent tutoring systems. Jia et al. (2024) compared ChatGPT-generated tips with human-crafted ones for solving mathematical problems. Among 121 evaluators, human tips were rated significantly higher, though evaluators could not reliably distinguish between human and AI tips. While ChatGPT's tips were innovative, they often lacked accuracy and relevance, with fewer than 40% considered relevant. Similarly, Pardos and Bhandari (2023) found that 70% of ChatGPT-generated hints passed quality checks, and both human and AI hints led to positive learning gains, but only gains from human-created hints were statistically significant. \n\nOur review for literature highlights ChatGPT's dual role in mathematics education as both a promising supplementary tool and one with notable limitations for complex subjects and higher learning levels. While acknowledging positive effects on student engagement and learning, we capture a crucial research gap: the exploration of how mathematics teachers' beliefs and practices align with the perceptions of the potential of ChatGPT in enhancing their teaching of mathematics. This gap, and the fact that beliefs are useful predictors of teachers' practices (Calleja, 2022;Cross, 2009), drives us towards the necessity for exploring the effectiveness of ChatGPT integration strategies in teaching mathematics and teachers' views of its benefits and challenges, drawing on their personal beliefs of mathematics, teaching, and learning. Realising that when it comes to changing classroom practices, teachers are the final policy brokers (McLaughlin, 1990), we think that attending to teachers' beliefs is essential to bridge the divide between technological adoption and pedagogical effectiveness in mathematics education that aims to optimise student engagement and learning.",
