      "snippet": {
        "text": "ChatGPT is a chatbot developed by OpenAI based on generative pre-trained transformer (GPT), a type of large language model, that learns from a vast amount of training data from different sources and generates outputs that are similar to the input training data. GPT was first developed in 2018, and ChatGPT, which was built on GPT-3.5, was released to the public for free in November 2022. In March 2023, GPT-4 was further released to the public via paid access. As an example of progress towards artificial general intelligence, where artificial intelligence (AI) systems perform intellectual tasks in a way that is indistinguishable from humans (Chan, 2023), ChatGPT is characterized as being able to generate human-like responses, converse with human users, and create textual content upon request. These characteristics afford ChatGPT great potential to be applied to aspects of contemporary work and lives, such as healthcare, translation, and programming (Stojanov, 2023), with significant practical and workforce implications in these areas. \n\nFor the higher education community, ChatGPT is arguably the most controversial, or even disruptive, technology that was made available in 2022 (Keiper et al., 2023). Since its release, there have been heated discussions, followed by preliminary exploration and evaluation, of ChatGPT's role and impact on core higher education activities. ChatGPT has infiltrated research and knowledge production processes, and suggestions for appropriate use have been put forward by the community (Stokel-Walker, 2023). In teaching and learning, educators have recognized ChatGPT's potential to allow personalized learning and timely feedback for students (Sallam, 2023) and facilitate course design and planning processes (Keiper, 2023). However, at the same time, they have identified various challenges associated with the application of ChatGPT. For example, Chat-GPT's human-like textual responses are difficult to differentiate from student work, which imposes challenges to academic integrity (Chan, 2023;Perkins & Roe, 2023) and forces universities and educators to reconsider assessment design (Nikolic et al., 2023).",
