          "disclaimer": "Notice: This snippet is extracted from the open access paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1007/s40751-024-00168-3?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1007/s40751-024-00168-3, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."
        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "The educational impact of GenAI technologies, such as ChatGPT, has received significant attention. The TPACK framework, developed by Mishra and Koehler (2006), is particularly useful for understanding how teachers integrate technology into their teaching. It emphasises the complex interactions between three primary forms of knowledge: content knowledge (CK), pedagogical knowledge (PK), and technological knowledge (TK). According to Mishra et al. (2023), TPACK highlights the qualities of GenAI that make it revolutionary, such as being generative and social, and explores how these traits affect specific knowledge domains. \n\nIn the context of this study, TPACK helps us to frame how mathematics teachers, based on their beliefs about mathematics, teaching, and learning, perceive and utilise ChatGPT within their pedagogical practices. As Lai et al. (2022) highlight, beliefs filter knowledge and guide its application, mediating how frameworks like TPACK translate into practice. These beliefs influence their TK, affecting their openness to integrating technology into their teaching. For instance, teachers with constructivist beliefs may be more inclined to explore how ChatGPT can facilitate student-centred learning, enhancing their integration of technology with pedagogy and content. \n\nExamining teachers' TK gives us insights into their familiarity and proficiency with ChatGPT. PK allows us to explore their teaching methods and how they integrate technology to engage students while CK ensures that the use of Chat-GPT aligns with the subject matter being taught. Teachers' practices reflect their ability to integrate technology into their pedagogy and content delivery effectively. Within the TPACK framework, a teacher's use of ChatGPT to generate mathematical problems demonstrates the integration of TK with CK, while using it to facilitate collaborative learning activities involves integrating TK with PK. \n\nThe TPACK framework supports our investigation into how teachers' beliefs about ChatGPT influence their engagement strategies and instructional practices in the mathematics classroom. It provides a structured approach to analyse the potential of ChatGPT to enhance student learning and engagement while also acknowledging the challenges and limitations highlighted in the literature (Mishra et al., 2023).",
