      "snippet": {
        "text": "A variety of tasks is used to investigate the pre-service teachers' use and technique for prompting ChatGPT, which has provoked different rates of erroneous answers produced by the model. As we are interested in pre-service teachers' ability to recognize mistakes in answers provided by ChatGPT, tasks are required which the chatbot is unlikely to solve correctly. Since answers can only be reproduced to a certain extent, something like the \"likelihood for correct solution\" is hard to grasp for large language models like ChatGPT. To get an impression of the behavior, the authors let the chatbot solve the tasks on multiple occasions to get an idea of this likelihood. One clue that we do have, however, is that the large language model seems to have trouble with mathematical problems as used, e.g., in mathematics Olympiads (Frieder et al., 2023). To make sure that the participants are able to identify the mistakes, tasks were chosen which can be solved with basic mathematical knowledge relevant for teaching in lower secondary schools. Based on these criteria (task variety, likelihood of correct answers, target audience of students), four tasks were chosen (Table 1). \n\nThe first task is concerned with the greatest common divisor and can be solved algorithmically, which is why it seems likely that the chatbot solves it adequately. This matches the trials performed prior to the data collection. Tasks about the greatest common divisors can be found in many curricula in different countries, especially of lower secondary schools. Regardless of the procedure of finding the solution, the pre-service teachers should be able to understand it-if presented by the chatbot adequately. Due to the nature of large language models, further inquiry is possible. \n\nThe second task was published as part of training tasks for mathematical competitions (Andrews et al., 2023). It can simply be solved by creating permutations of possible numbers. What makes it especially interesting is that it defines zebra numbers, which are not a common concept in mathematics. This definition is vital for finding a solution requiring the chatbot to make proper use of it. A correct solution did not appear likely to the authors prior to data collection.",
