      "snippet": {
        "text": "The sample for the current study included middle school mathematics classrooms in the Northeast, Mid-Atlantic, and Western regions of the United States. Teachers were recruited via e-mail blasts to field interest in assigning the study's measures as homework to their students with parental consent. Based on how data were collected using the online tutoring site ASSISTments (Heffernan & Heffernan, 2014), all data were anonymized to protect students' identities. Thus, there is no information available about the individual schools in this study, and this information was not controlled for. Thus, the IRB for this study was considered exempt due to the anonymous nature of the data and because the activities were considered normal classroom research. However, this study's sample included 11 math teachers and their students. \n\nParticipants (N = 114) from over 11 classrooms completed the pretest for the study, with n = 73 completing the study. The overall sample included White/European American (38%), Black/African American (44%), Asian (7.6%), Latine (5.1%), and Middle Eastern/North African (5.1%). The sample also included children with mothers who had earned at least a graduate or professional degree (37.7%), at least a Bachelor's degree (19.5%), had completed at least some college or community college (13%), a high school diploma (10.4%), or a mother that did not graduate from high school (3.9%); 15.6% of the sample did not know how far their mother had gone in school. Mother's education was used as a proxy for socio-economic status. The sample was largely male (60%) and overwhelmingly possessed English as a first language (96.2%). The mean age was 11.7 years. Students were assigned at random to one of the four conditions: Problem Solving or Business as Usual (n = 16), Worked Example with Self Explanation (n = 20), Fading (n = 22), and Fading with Self Explanation (n = 15).",
