      "snippet": {
        "text": "Recently, there has been widespread attention to research on AI-based generative conversational models like Chat-GPT. A study conducted by Abdulhadi Shoufan explored students' perceptions of ChatGPT, with students expressing appreciation for its conversational chat functionality, finding it interesting, inspiring, and helpful for learning and work. However, many students also recognize that using ChatGPT requires a good background knowledge as it cannot replace human intelligence. ChatGPT can and should be used for learning purposes, but students need to be aware of its limitations. Educators should try using ChatGPT and provide students with appropriate techniques for using it effectively, as well as helping them evaluate the generated responses [3]. From the perspectives of both students and educators, Md. Mostafifizer Rahman and others discussed the potential opportunities and challenges of ChatGPT in education. The ChatGPT model can be applied to solve technical problems (such as engineering and computer programming) as well as non-technical problems (such as language and literature). However, it also faces challenges such as a lack of common sense, potential biases, difficulties in complex reasoning, and the inability to handle visual information. It is important to bear these limitations in mind when using ChatGPT and avoid blindly relying on these technologies. Immediate efforts should be made to address these disadvantages to ensure fairness for all [4]. Taking the teaching of quadratic equations in junior high school as an example, You Jiajing and others analyzed the opportunities and challenges of applying ChatGPT in the process of learning junior high school mathematics. They concluded that the ChatGPT model has the potential to assist students in learning junior high school mathematics, especially when teachers are involved. However, in the absence of teacher guidance, most students still struggle to directly engage in mathematical learning through interaction with ChatGPT. As AI technology continues to advance, it can provide a new approach to promoting educational equity [5]. \n\nThe Rasch model has been widely employed in various fields, including education and psychology, and has matured as a research tool. It has demonstrated considerable utility in the context of exercise testing. For instance, Amir Mohamed Talib and colleagues utilized the Rasch model to assess the performance of students in the Information Technology Foundation (IT280) course's final exam at an Arab Muslim University.",
