        "text": "This study, while providing valuable insights into the perceptions and applications of ChatGPT in mathematics education, has limitations. Conducted among secondary school mathematics teachers in Malta, the findings may not be generalisable to other regions or educational levels. Malta's unique cultural, pedagogical, and technological contexts may not be entirely applicable elsewhere. \n\nThe reliance on self-reported data through questionnaires and field notes may introduce biases related to self-perception and social desirability. Teachers might report a more optimistic view of their willingness to integrate technology or a skewed perception of their beliefs and teaching practices. Additionally, the rapid evolution of AI tools like ChatGPT could render some findings outdated as new features are introduced. Moreover, the study's focus on teacher perceptions rather than direct classroom observations leaves a gap in understanding the actual impact of ChatGPT on student engagement, learning outcomes, and classroom dynamics. \n\nTo address these limitations, future research should include comparative studies across different cultural and educational contexts to understand how various environments influence the adoption and impact of ChatGPT. Such research could reveal the role of educational policies, teacher PD programmes, and cultural attitudes towards technology. \n\nLongitudinal studies tracking ChatGPT's adoption and effects over time would provide insights into how teachers' perceptions evolve and the technology's longterm impact on student outcomes and teaching practices. Classroom observations and analysis of student work could complement self-reported data, offering a fuller picture of ChatGPT's practical use and direct effects on teaching and learning. \n\nIt is important to acknowledge the abovementioned limitations in our research design and methodology. We think that relying on self-reported data without direct classroom observations does not fully capture students' actual engagement with Chat-GPT. Because of this, we chose a relatively broad definition where engagement is seen as focusing on the time and effort students dedicate to learning activities (Radloff & Coates, 2010). While this definition seemed appropriate for the study of teachers' perceptions on the engagement affordability by ChatGPT, it does not capture the full complexity of engagement as described by more comprehensive frameworks which focus on observable student activities. Goldin et al. (2011) propose a richer framework of engagement structures, including cognitive, affective, and social components.",
