      "snippet": {
        "text": "Artificial intelligence (AI) plays a pivotal role in our daily activities. Every search on our preferred search engine, email spam filtering, or image editing task involves AI to some degree, as these technologies aim to execute 'optimal action' (Russell & Norvig, 2021). The emergence of generative AI (GenAI) highlights the growing importance of AI in our lives. Using GenAI, machines trained on vast datasets perform tasks that humans cannot achieve within the same timeframe or at all. GenAI utilises deep learning technology to automatically generate text, images, audio, video, and various other forms of content (Moorhouse et al., 2023). \n\nIn November 2022, OpenAI launched a ground-breaking GenAI-powered chatbot called ChatGPT, which is underpinned by a large language model (LLM). The rapid embrace of GenAI technologies such as ChatGPT and their media portrayal are expected to shape future educational methods, not least in the changing role of teachers (Stokel-Walker, 2022). Despite its potential in mathematics, ChatGPT has limitations in executing calculations (Frieder et al., 2023) and correcting geometry misconceptions (Wardat et al., 2023). However, despite these shortcomings the logic behind the solutions offered for analytical problems is typically sound (Mustapha et al., 2024), although other research highlights significant limitations in its ability to consistently provide sound logical reasoning for complex mathematical tasks (Frieder et al., 2023;Wardat et al., 2023). \n\nMathematics teachers, as the principal facilitators of learning in the mathematics classroom, exert considerable influence over adopting and effectively utilising technologies like ChatGPT within the classroom (Patero, 2023). This influence is shaped by their knowledge and awareness of the potential of ChatGPT but also by their beliefs and practices. For the scope of this paper, we define beliefs as personal thoughts, opinions and theories about curriculum, teaching and learning, that act as mental filters through which teachers interpret new knowledge and its potential use in the classroom (Calleja, 2022).",
