      "snippet": {
        "text": "By tracing student progress in this model, the software is able to intervene with well-targeted feedback when the student makes errors, and to continuously update its assessment of skill mastery by considering the student's correct actions, errors, and hint requests. The software's adaptive nature is designed to provide individualized instruction to address students' specific needs. The multistep problems provide opportunities for students to build on prior knowledge and progress from concrete to abstract thinking. The software uses multiple representations including diagrams, equations, and text and encourages students to engage in a variety of strategies to solve problems, such as breaking an unfamiliar problem down into simpler problems they already know how to solve. \n\nAlong with the tutorial software, the curriculum provides student and teacher textbooks and workbooks, as well as detailed guidelines for instructors on how to organize classroom time. Typically, teachers receive three days of training before starting to use the curriculum. They also receive an additional day of follow-up training after they begin implementation. This training includes instruction on both the software and the classroom-based activities. \n\nCognitive Tutor curricula have shown promise for raising student achievement in quasi-experimental and experimental field studies. In particular, the Cognitive Tutor Algebra I curriculum, which uses the same computer-based tutoring approach as Cognitive Tutor Geometry, demonstrated positive effects on students' mathematics learning in a randomized efficacy experiment (Ritter et al., 2007). Quasi-experimental studies evaluating the Algebra I curriculum in Texas, Miami-Dade County, and Pittsburgh/Western Pennsylvania also found that students who received the Cognitive Tutor curriculum showed higher algebra achievement and attitudes toward mathematics than their counterparts who received these districts' standard curricula (Carnegie Learning, 2001aLearning, , 2001bLearning, , 2001cLearning, , 2002;;Koedinger, Anderson, Hadley, & Mark, 1997;Sarkis, 2004). However, a 2-year experiment sponsored by the U.S. Department of Education, which evaluated Cognitive Tutor Algebra I among ten reading and mathematics software products, did not find positive results for that curriculum (Campuzano, Dynarski, Agodini, & Rall, 2009).",
