      "snippet": {
        "text": "The search strategy followed the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analysis (PRISMA) statement when selecting relevant articles (Moher et al., 2009). It included a three-step procedure (von Elm et al., 2019). In the initial step, databases and search terms for the literature search were selected, and then certain criteria for the inclusion and exclusion of literature were defined (see Snyder, 2019). We used Google Scholar and the Web of Science as databases, as both are particularly comprehensive and provide both national and international literature. We also added registers such as ArXiv and HAL in the search, as this is where we found literature that has not yet been published in peer-reviewed journals due to the timeliness of the research. \n\nIn the second step of the search strategy, the databases were searched using the search strings \"ChatGPT,\" \"GPT,\" and \"Mathematics\" (including the suffix \"educ*\"), as well as \"teach*\" and \"learn*.\" Search strings that represent superordinate categories to ChatGPT (such as \"artificial intelligence,\" \"large language models,\" \"machine learning\") were excluded, as they increased the definitional vagueness of our research question, which was restricted to ChatGPT in mathematics education. This was done primarily for practical reasons: Whilst investigating resources and tools used by students for their learning of mathematics in engineering and teacher education (Pepin & Kock, 2021;Kock, Salinas-Hernández, and Pepin this issue), ChatGPT emerged as one of the most frequently used AI tools. Other AI technologies or large language models such as Claude or Gemini were also excluded; it is not the aim of this review to distinguish between different AI language models used in studies on mathematical teaching and learning. Their exclusion does not imply irrelevance but rather reflects the practical need to align the Special Issue with the predominant tool in current educational discourse. Furthermore, ChatGPT serves as an exemplar of how generative AI models can impact mathematics education, making it a suitable case for in-depth analysis. Non-academic articles (e.g., articles from mass and social media) were also excluded. \n\nWe limited the time frame of our search from November 2022 to May 2024.",
