      "snippet": {
        "text": "The PD program was designed to improve middle school mathematics teachers' CK and PCK of ratios and proportional relationships, a critical content area in the middle school mathematics curriculum (Siegler et al., 2010;Thompson & Saldanha, 2003). The content of the program consists of two modules targeting CK and PCK, respectively. The CK module has five submodules with a total of 35 activities to enhance teachers' understanding of the key concepts related to ratios and proportional rela-tionships. The PCK module includes three submodules (i.e., planning, implementing, and assessing and reflecting on a lesson) with 18 multistage activities, such as selecting and adapting tasks that are aligned with the learning goals, analyzing students' work and responses, and reflecting on the affordances and limitations of instructional strategies (see Table 1 for content details). We deliberately focused on enhancing both the CK and PCK of teachers because prior systematic reviews have shown that PD programs targeting both CK and PCK yield more effective outcomes than addressing them separately (Kennedy, 1998;Scher & O'Reilly, 2009). We used classroom artifacts, such as student work samples and video clips of mathematics instruction, based on evidence in recent reviews showing that using classroom artifacts to engage teachers in active learning produced larger program impacts (Kennedy, 2019). \n\nThe PD program operates in a system adapted from one version of a dialoguebased intelligent tutoring system (ITS; e.g., Falmagne et al., 2013;Graesser, 2011;VanLehn et al., 2010). As teachers interact with the learning activities, a learner model in the system diagnoses teachers' knowledge through their responses to a problem, followed by a tutor model that provides adaptive feedback when a teacher cannot provide an appropriate response to the problem (for a detailed description of the program design, see Copur-Gencturk & Orrill, 2023;Copur-Gencturk, Li, & Atabas, 2024).