        "text": "In the context of student simulation, prior research indicates that even the most advanced LLMs struggle to follow counterfactual instructions when simulating lower-performing student personas [47], and LLMs struggle more in responding to children compared with responding to adults [29]. Several possible reasons for the misalignment in simulation have been identified, including distributional biases in pretraining data [88] and the fact that LLMs are generally optimized to behave and communicate in the style of helpful assistants, rather than adopting the diverse patterns of real human users [16]. \n\nOne potential direction is to employ an open-weight LLM and fine-tune or post-train it on youth dialogues [88,111]. However, a critical challenge here is the lack of middle school classroom datasets that include mathematical CPS dialogues. Existing educational resources cover adjacent areas, such as teacher-student tutoring sessions [54,62], classroom transcripts [93], and child-caregiver interactions [56], but they do not target peer CPS, which calls for community effort to collect more for educational applications that support CPS.",
