      "snippet": {
        "text": "The data for this study were collected as part of a research project that supports middle school teachers to incorporate problem posing in their classrooms through a multiyear intensive professional development program. The three project schools were all located in the mid-Atlantic region of the United States, and each was identified as a high-needs school during the project, with 20-35% of the students identified as multilingual learners. In the program, teachers engage in problem-posing activities, learn routines for implementing problem-posing learning models, and design problem-posing tasks to use in their classrooms. The present study drew on participating teachers' student assessment data from two problem-posing tasks, the Road Trip task and the Doorbell task. The Road Trip task is as follows: Jerome, Elliot, and Arturo took turns driving home from a trip. Arturo drove 80 miles more than Elliot. Elliot drove twice as many miles as Jerome. Jerome drove 50 miles. The Doorbell task is as follows: Sally is having a party. The first time the doorbell rings, 1 guest enters. The second time the doorbell rings, 3 guests enter. The third time the doorbell rings, 5 guests enter. The fourth time the doorbell rings, 7 guests enter. This pattern continues in the same way. On the next ring, a group enters that has 2 more guests than the group that entered on the previous ring. \n\nFor each task, one of two prompts was randomly given: \"Pose three different mathematical problems that could be answered using the given information\" (Pose-3) and \"Pose one easy, one somewhat difficult, and one difficult problem that could be answered using the given information\" (Pose-EMD). In total, 266 middle school students completed the two tasks, generating 1,596 responses with 279 unexpected responses (accounting for 17.48% of the responses; see Table 1). This study focuses on students' unexpected responses that were not solvable mathematical problems based on the following analytical processes.",
