      "snippet": {
        "text": "The emergence of artificial intelligence (AI) and, in particular, large language models such as ChatGPT (Zhang, Wang, Huang, Zhang, Chen and Dong, 2023) has introduced numerous opportunities and challenges in the field of education. Built on the GPT (generative pre-trained transformer) architecture (Brown, Mann, Ryder, Subbiah, Kaplan, Dhariwal, Neelakantan, Shyam, Sastry, Askell et al., 2020), ChatGPT is capable of generating human-like text and has been used for various tasks across marketing, medicine, and other domains (Guo, Chen, Zhang, Liu, Wang and Huang, 2023). The potential of ChatGPT in promoting personalized and interactive learning, enhancing formative assessment practices, and facilitating research and teaching has been highlighted by several studies (Alser and Waisberg, 2023;Baidoo-Anu and Owusu Ansah, 2023;Halaweh, 2023;Kasneci et al., 2023;Sok and Heng, 2023). Chat-GPT has been employed in various educational contexts, including medical education (Kung et al., 2023;Lee, 2023), computer science (Bordt and von Luxburg, 2023), and mathematics (Frieder et al., 2023), and the list will undoubtedly grow exponentially. Through these applications, the model has demonstrated the potential to improve student engagement, interaction, and personalized learning experiences (Kasneci et al., 2023). \n\nDespite its massive successes, ChatGPT has also fallen short of its hype in some domains, with limitations in mathematical abilities (Frieder et al., 2023), reasoning, and factual accuracy (Borji, 2023). Importantly for this work, studies have shown that ChatGPT may not be sophisticated enough to provide meaningful feedback to students (King and chatGPT, 2023) or to detect sophisticated plagiarism techniques (Khalil and Er, 2023).",
