          "status": "GOLD",
          "disclaimer": "Notice: This snippet is extracted from the open access paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.5642/jhummath.orjj7193?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.5642/jhummath.orjj7193, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."
        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "For a long time, students, teachers, parents, and researchers have observed clear differences between mathematical practices in schools and those in everyday life. First, school-based mathematical practices are driven by school curricula or teacher-imposed requirements, while out-of-school practices are driven by individual needs and real-life events. Second, motivation for engaging in school mathematics is typically extrinsic, whereas out-of-school mathematical engagement tends to be arise from intrinsic motivations. In schools, mathematical practices and their applications are shaped by teachers or curricula, whereas outside of school, they are driven by individuals within the context of sociocultural norms. Additionally, in schools, discourse around mathematical practices follows structured routines, while outside of school, it is often more fluid and negotiated. \n\nVarious theoretical frameworks have been used to explain differences between in-school and out-of-school mathematical practices. This article draws on Cultural Historical Activity Theory (CHAT), particularly the construct of the activity system in CHAT, along with the notion of the boundary object, to explore the interface between school-based and out-of-school mathematical practices. \n\nAt this point, it is reasonable to suggest that ChatGPT, a generative conversational AI model, has the potential to act as a boundary object that can bridge in-school and out-of-school mathematical practices. Its conversational capabilities, designed to engage users in natural, coherent, and contextually relevant dialogue, may facilitate the interaction between these different practice environments. However, ChatGPT remains a developing, black-boxed project whose reliability depends on the vast corpus of textual data used in its training. Moreover, various individual, sociocultural, and sociopolitical factors can influence the quality of its educational outputs. Among these, human agency plays a crucial role, as the way human users interact with ChatGPT in educational settings significantly influences its effectiveness. For instance, when used as a negotiation tool through dialogue, it promotes a different kind of learning compared to when it is employed solely as a source of direct answers. \n\nAn emerging trend conceptualizes numeracy as a social practice, focusing on \"what people do with numeracy through social interactions in specific contexts, rather than assessing their performance of mathematical skills in isolation\"; see for example [8].",
