        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "The literature suggests that AI technology is different and should not be compared to existing technology, and that the community needs to learn how to employ this technology in a safe and appropriate way. \n\nMoreover, we argue that the identified features of ChatGPT allow students to have more agency over their mathematics learning (for digital resources and agency, see, for example, Pepin et al., 2024b, or Salinas-Hernández et al., 2023): they can access mathematical content and solutions to particular problems \"in time\" when they need them for their projects; they can discuss their problems with ChatGPT and be tutored as needed. At the same time, we suggest that the learner-tool relationship changes: whereas before the (non-AI-driven) tool was used by the learnerits possibilities were \"static\" and pre-defined, now the (AI-driven) tool has become \"dynamic\"-its possibilities develop as it has its own \"intelligence.\" This provides agency also for the AI tool, and the tool becomes an \"agentic partner\" for the student in their learning of mathematics, continuously adapting and responding to the evolving needs of the learner. This shift not only alters the way students interact with educational tools, but also redefines the role of technology in shaping personalized learning experiences, ultimately encouraging a more interactive and responsive educational environment.",
