        "text": "We controlled for numerous student-and school-level covariates in our models. Each variable is measured annually for each student in grades 3 through 8; we used the measures from the 7th-grade wave, which corresponds with the timing of decisions about 8th-grade math placements. Student-level demographic controls include the following dichotomous variables: sex (male versus female), free and reduced-price lunch status (FRPL), English language learner (ELL) status, and special education status (SPED). We also control for cohort effects by including indicator variables for the year students began 7th grade. \n\nPast research consistently indicates the importance of prior achievement for course placements. Our measure of prior achievement is the student's 7th-grade math score on the Indiana Statewide Testing for Educational Progress-Plus (ISTEP+) assessment. The ISTEP+ is the standardized accountability test in Indiana, which is taken in the spring of each academic year. It is reported as a scale score, which allows for comparisons among all students in the state (the grand achievement queue). To measure students' standing within the achievement queue in their school, we created a school mean-centered measure of 7th-grade math achievement. We adjust for possible measurement error in math  scores by including an additional control for students' 7th-grade English Language Arts (ELA) ISTEP+ performance. To ease interpretation, we standardized both math and ELA test scores for our regression analyses. Finally, we include 7th-grade test scores in our regression using a quadratic specification, which allows for nonlinear effects in predicting the outcomes. 8  We also include a second measure of a student's math ability in our models: whether a student was identified by school personnel as \"high ability,\" or gifted, in math in 7th grade. High ability status is conferred upon students using a multifaceted student assessment plan; it is locally determined by each school, and students are identified to be high ability in math if they perform at or above the 96th percentile of a norm-referenced measure of math ability (which is chosen by the school). For middle school students, parent and teacher recommendations and 6th-grade ISTEP+ scores are used to determine which students are given the high-ability assessment.",
