      "snippet": {
        "text": "It is important to acknowledge SLDs' difficulties, and also highlight the successes they experience. As researchers, we should have a critical eye toward how SLDs are discussed in literature and what we can do to change the narrative (Lambert et al., 2018;Yeh et al., 2020). Lambert investigated the experiences of two middle school SLDs in a mathematics classroom (Lambert, 2015). Mathematics instruction in this classroom shifted from open-ended problemsolving tasks at the beginning to more structured memorization toward the end of one year. Lambert sought to examine how the pedagogical shifts might shape students' learning experiences. One student maintained a fixed idea that she was not as smart as her peers and that explicit instruction was most helpful. The other student maintained his opinion that open discussion and persistence in math was important to success. Although these two students maintained different beliefs about mathematics learning, these beliefs help teachers and researchers foster students' success in mathematics (Lambert, 2015). \n\nA more recent study explored typical interventions for three fifth-grade SLDs in mathematics classrooms (Wilson & Hunt, 2022). Some typical interventions that have showed promise for promoting success among SLDs include think alouds and small-group instruction. The researchers aimed to qualitatively examine if these interventions promoted engagement from SLDs in problem solving and sensemaking. Findings suggested that interventions aimed at drawing out problem-solving behaviors from SLDs provided opportunities for students to showcase their abilities. Considering past scholarship, it becomes evident that SLDs may think about mathematics differently and can contribute different ways of thinking when given the chance, through activities like think alouds. An implication for the present research is that tools, like think alouds, empower students' voice and agency during mathematical problem solving while 